    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Host Information — Software", "technique": "T1592.002", "what_happened": "At 2027-08-14 10:02:11 CEST, Suricata logged a burst of NMAP service-version-detection alerts ('ET SCAN Nmap Scripting Engine User-Agent Detected' and 'ET SCAN Suspicious inbound to mySQL port 3306', signature pattern consistent with nmap -sV --version-intensity 7) with data.srcip=45.137.21.66 targeting a SINGLE host data.dstip=10.108.57.198 across 21 well-known service ports (21,22,25,53,80,110,135,139,143,443,445,587,993,995,3306,3389,5985,5986,8080,8443). This is NOT a subnet sweep — it is a deep single-host software-version enumeration. The attacker is reading the EXACT version string of every exposed daemon. ~10 minutes duration.", "chain_reason": "Suricata signatures grouped under rule.groups containing 'suricata' + 'ids' + 'scan' fired with a single data.srcip=45.137.21.66 hitting >15 distinct data.dstport values on the same data.dstip=10.108.57.198 within a 10-minute window — the univariate fingerprint of nmap -sV intense version detection against one target.", "first_seen": "2027-08-14T10:02:11+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Host Information — Banner Grab", "technique": "T1592.002", "what_happened": "At 10:14:30 CEST (~3-minute pause after the nmap -sV phase ended), the same data.srcip=45.137.21.66 opened a sequence of raw TCP connections to data.dstport in {21,22,25,80,110,143,443,3389} on data.dstip=10.108.57.198, each connection ~35 seconds apart — the deliberate human-typing cadence of manual netcat banner grabbing. Suricata fired 'ET SCAN Netcat Banner Grab' / non-browser raw-socket signatures. Protocol-specific probe payloads were sent (FTP HELP, SMTP EHLO, IMAP CAPABILITY) to coax verbose version banners from each daemon. ~6 minutes duration.", "chain_reason": "Suricata raw-socket / netcat banner-grab signatures with data.srcip=45.137.21.66 against data.dstip=10.108.57.198 on 8 distinct data.dstport values, each ~35s apart — too slow and too sequential for automation, the operator-typing cadence of manual nc -nv probing. The ~3-minute inter-phase pause between Step 1 (nmap -sV) and Step 2 (manual banner grab) is the analyst-CVE-matching signature of a competent human operator.", "first_seen": "2027-08-14T10:14:30+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Gather Victim Host Information — Software (HTTP fingerprint)", "technique": "T1592.002", "what_happened": "At 10:23:05 CEST (~3-minute pause after Step 2), the same data.srcip=45.137.21.66 issued a series of HTTP HEAD requests to data.dstip=10.108.57.198 on data.dstport=80 against the paths / /robots.txt /server-status /api /api/v1 /favicon.ico /sitemap.xml. Each request rotated its User-Agent across four realistic Mozilla-style strings (Chrome/Firefox/Safari profiles) and was spaced ~25 seconds apart. Suricata fired 'ET INFO HTTP Server header information disclosure' and 'ET POLICY curl/non-standard client' style signatures. The attacker is fingerprinting the web server product, version and framework from the Server: and X-Powered-By: response headers. ~5 minutes duration.", "chain_reason": "Suricata HTTP-header-disclosure / policy signatures with data.srcip=45.137.21.66 to data.dstip=10.108.57.198 data.dstport=80, 7 sequential HEAD requests with rotating User-Agent, ~25s apart. The UA rotation is a deliberate low-noise evasion technique — a competent operator varying client fingerprints to blend with normal traffic.", "first_seen": "2027-08-14T10:23:05+0200"},
